<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec3-12" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Section 3.1/3.2</title>
  <definition xml:id="def-quadratic">
    <title>Quadratic Function</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>quadratic function</term> is one which can be written in the form
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c </mrow>
        </md>
        where <m>a\neq 0</m>, but <m>b</m> or <m>c</m> could be <m>0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The shape of a quadratic function is called a <term>parabola</term>, shown in the figure below. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="sec3-12-quadratic-fig1">
    <caption>Graph of a quadratic function</caption>
    <image xml:id="sec3-12-quadratic-fig1img1" width = "50%">
      <description><p>
        The graph of a quadratic function.
      </p></description>
      <sageplot>
        p = plot(4*x - x^2, xmin = -1, xmax = 5, axes_labels=['$x$','$f(x)$'])
        p
      </sageplot>
    </image>
  </figure>
  
  <definition xml:id="def-vertex">
    <title>Vertex</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>vertex</term> of a parabola is the point where the graph turns from down to up or from up to down.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <assemblage xml:id="sec3-12-quadratic-props">
    <title>Properties of Quadratic Functions</title>
    <p>
      Recall that a quadratic function takes the form <m>y = ax^2 + bx + c</m>
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>a \gt 0</m>, the parabola opens upward, and the vertex is the <alert>absolute minimum</alert> of the function.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>a \lt 0</m>, the parabola opens downward, and the vertex is the <alert>absolute maximum</alert> of the function.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The vertex is located at the point
            <md>
              <mrow> \lrpar{-\dfrac{b}{2a},f\lrpar{-\dfrac{b}{2a}}} </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Quadratics can have two, one, or zero real <xref ref="def-rootszerossolutions" text="custom">roots</xref>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <example xml:id="sec3-12-quadratic-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="def-costrevprofit"/> that revenue can be found by multiplying the price per unit times the number of units.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the price of a commodity is given by <m>P(x) = -0.1x + 600</m> dollars per unit, then the revenue is found by multiplying 
        <md>
          <mrow> R(x) = x\cdot P(x) = -0.1x^2 + 600x </mrow>
        </md>       
      </p>
      <p>
        The vertex can be found by using the formula from <xref ref="sec3-12-quadratic-props" text="title"/>:
        <md>
          <mrow> x \amp = -\dfrac{b}{2a} </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp = -\dfrac{600}{2(-0.1)} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 3000 </mrow>
        </md>
        So the input of the vertex is at <m>3000</m>. The output is at <m>f\lrpar{-\dfrac{b}{2a}}</m>, so it is at <m>f(3000)</m>, which we can compute to be <m>900000</m>. So, the vertex is at <m>(3000,900000)</m>; we can interpret this to say that when <m>3000</m> units are sold, the revenue is <m>900,000</m> dollars
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="sec3-12-quadratic-cu1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The percentage of foreign-born people in the United States was determined to be modeled by the equation <m>y = 0.0018x^2 - 0.216x + 13.0</m> percent, where <m>x</m> is the number of years after 1910.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Determine the year, according to the model, that the number of foreign-born persons was at a minimum. If necessary, round to the nearest whole number.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What is the minimum percentage?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              60 years after 1910, or 1970.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>6.52%</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <definition xml:id="def-quadratic-equation">
    <title>Quadratic Equation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>quadratic equation</term> is an equation which can be written as
        <md>
          <mrow> ax^2 + bx + c = 0 </mrow>
        </md>
        where <m>a\neq 0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-quadratic-formula">
    <title>Quadratic Formula</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a quadratic equation of the form <m>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</m>, the solution(s) to the equation are given by 
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp = \dfrac{-b + \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} </mrow>
        </md>
        and
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp = \dfrac{-b - \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="sec3-12-quadraticformula-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the quadratic equation <m>4x - 3x^2= -6</m>, we can find its real roots by first making it look like the equation <m>ax^2 + bx + c = 0</m>. Rewriting, we have
        <md>
          <mrow> -3x^2 + 4x + 6 = 0</mrow>
        </md>
        Now, <m>a = -3</m>, <m>b = 4</m>, and <m>c = 6</m>. Plugging this into the quadratic formula, we get 
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp = \dfrac{-b + \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp = \dfrac{-4 + \sqrt{(4)^2-4(-3)(6)}}{2(-3)} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp  = \dfrac{-4+\sqrt{88}}{-6}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp \approx 2.230 </mrow>
        </md>
        and
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp = \dfrac{-b - \sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp = \dfrac{-4 - \sqrt{(4)^2-4(-3)(6)}}{2(-3)} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp  = \dfrac{-4-\sqrt{88}}{-6}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp \approx -0.897 </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="sec3-12-quadraticformula-cu1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the real root(s) of the equation <m>4x^2 + 20x = -25</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        The only real root is at <m>x = -2.5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</section>